# Database PII Encryption Patterns

## Required Pattern for PII Fields

All PII (Personally Identifiable Information) fields MUST be encrypted using PostgreSQL's `pgp_sym_encrypt()` and `pgp_sym_decrypt()` functions.

### INSERT/UPDATE Pattern (Encryption)
```sql
CASE 
    WHEN sqlc.narg(pii_field) IS NOT NULL 
    THEN pgp_sym_encrypt(sqlc.narg(pii_field), sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
    ELSE NULL 
END::varchar
```

### SELECT Pattern (Decryption)  
```sql
CASE 
    WHEN pii_field IS NOT NULL 
    THEN pgp_sym_decrypt(pii_field::bytea, sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
    ELSE NULL 
END::text as pii_field
```

## Critical Type Safety Rule

⚠️ **ALWAYS cast CASE expressions with `::varchar` or `::text`** to prevent sqlc from generating `interface{}` types. 

Reference: [sqlc issue #2937](https://github.com/sqlc-dev/sqlc/issues/2937)

## PII Fields in DECM

**Authentication Credentials:**
- `google_connector_ref`
- `github_connector_ref`

**Profiles:** 
- `profile_picture_url`
- `first_name`, `last_name` 
- `email`
- `bio`
- `phone_number`
- `address`
- `academic_institution`
- `academic_email`

## Required Parameters

All encrypted queries MUST include:
- `encryption_key` parameter using `sqlc.arg(encryption_key)`
- Use `sqlc.narg()` for nullable PII fields
- Use `sqlc.arg()` for required fields

## Example Implementation

```sql
-- name: CreateProfile :one
INSERT INTO profiles (first_name) VALUES (
    CASE 
        WHEN sqlc.narg(first_name) IS NOT NULL 
        THEN pgp_sym_encrypt(sqlc.narg(first_name), sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
        ELSE NULL 
    END::varchar
) RETURNING 
    CASE 
        WHEN first_name IS NOT NULL 
        THEN pgp_sym_decrypt(first_name::bytea, sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
        ELSE NULL 
    END::text as first_name;
```